Excellent question — this is a **very common task** for NixOS users who want SSH keys to be available automatically (for Git, remote servers, etc.).

Let’s go through the different ways to **enable `ssh-agent`** and **load your SSH keys** in your NixOS `configuration.nix` — both for **system-wide** and **per-user** setups.

---

## 🧩 1. Background — how `ssh-agent` works

`ssh-agent` is a small background process that holds your private keys in memory and provides them to `ssh`, `git`, etc., when needed.
Normally you start it when your session begins (via PAM, a desktop session, or manually).

NixOS can manage this for you.

---

## 🧰 2. The simple way: use `programs.ssh.startAgent`

In your `/etc/nixos/configuration.nix`, add:

```nix
{
  programs.ssh = {
    startAgent = true;
    extraConfig = ''
      AddKeysToAgent yes
    '';
  };
}
```

### What this does:

* Starts an `ssh-agent` automatically when you log in (for all users, unless overridden).
* The `AddKeysToAgent yes` line tells `ssh` to automatically add your key to the agent when used.

Then rebuild your configuration:

```bash
sudo nixos-rebuild switch
```

---

## 🔑 3. Automatically add a key (using user’s configuration)

If you want a specific key to be added automatically, you can do this via `environment.variables` or user shell initialization.

For example:

```nix
{
  programs.ssh = {
    startAgent = true;
  };

  # Optional: make sure SSH_AUTH_SOCK is visible in all shells
  environment.variables.SSH_AUTH_SOCK = "/run/user/${toString config.users.users.youruser.uid}/ssh-agent";

  users.users.youruser = {
    isNormalUser = true;
    extraGroups = [ "wheel" ];
    shell = pkgs.zsh;
  };
}
```

Then, inside your user’s shell (e.g. `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`):

```bash
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

You can make this automatic with Nix if you use **home-manager** (see below).

---

## 🏠 4. The Home Manager way (recommended for per-user setup)

If you use **Home Manager** (which integrates nicely with NixOS), it can manage the agent and your keys declaratively:

```nix
{
  programs.ssh = {
    enable = true;
    startAgent = true;
    forwardAgent = true;

    # Automatically add keys when home-manager session starts
    extraConfig = ''
      AddKeysToAgent yes
      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    '';

    # Optional: preload certain keys
    matchBlocks = {
      "github.com" = {
        identityFile = "~/.ssh/id_ed25519";
      };
    };
  };
}
```

To actually add the key at login, Home Manager provides:

```nix
services.ssh-agent = {
  enable = true;
  identities = [ "~/.ssh/id_ed25519" ];
};
```

That tells Nix to start `ssh-agent` automatically and run `ssh-add` for that key on login.

Then rebuild:

```bash
home-manager switch
```

---

## ⚙️ 5. Example full NixOS + Home Manager integration

If you’re managing both through a flake, this might look like:

```nix
{
  description = "NixOS config with ssh-agent";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, home-manager, ... }: {
    nixosConfigurations.mysystem = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      modules = [
        ./hardware-configuration.nix
        {
          users.users.me = {
            isNormalUser = true;
            extraGroups = [ "wheel" "networkmanager" ];
          };

          programs.ssh.startAgent = true;
        }
      ];
    };

    homeConfigurations.me = home-manager.lib.homeManagerConfiguration {
      pkgs = import nixpkgs { system = "x86_64-linux"; };
      modules = [
        {
          services.ssh-agent = {
            enable = true;
            identities = [ "~/.ssh/id_ed25519" ];
          };
        }
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

---

## 🔍 6. Quick verification

After rebuilding:

```bash
echo $SSH_AUTH_SOCK
ssh-add -l
```

If `ssh-agent` is running, `$SSH_AUTH_SOCK` will point to a socket, and `ssh-add -l` will show your keys.

---

## ✅ Summary

| Goal                                      | Recommended method                                 |
| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Just want ssh-agent always running        | `programs.ssh.startAgent = true;`                  |
| Want specific keys auto-loaded (per-user) | Use Home Manager → `services.ssh-agent.identities` |
| Want declarative multi-user setup         | Combine both system and user-level configs         |
| Want to add keys interactively            | Use `ssh-add` manually in shell                    |

